summary.text:
Looking at SoftDeferBitBody apply via soft deferred implied - when it runs ExecuteOpcode BIT with Absolute(), PC is at opcode+1. But `_pc = opcode+1` and after soft apply ExecuteOpcode, PC advances to...I see the issue with the soft-deferred BIT Absolute() path. The current `_instructionPC` overwrite after the soft body is clobbering the correct PC for the next fetch, and the flag timing is off by one cycle in the host view.

The fix is to store the BIT value (and its flags) at soft-defer time, then apply them directly on soft apply without re-executing the full opcode. That keeps the PC correct (`_pc = opcodeAddress + 3`) and ensures the flags are visible to the host at the right moment.

Here's the updated SoftDeferBitBody and apply logic:

```csharp
 private byte _softBitOperand;
  private bool _bitSoftDeferEarly = true; // was (_opcode is 0x2C or 0x24) && _fetchAfterRtsDelay;

  private void SoftDeferBitBody()
  {
      _softBitOperand = Read(_pc); // operand 0x24 or 0x91
      _softDeferredImpliedOp = true;
      _softDeferredImpliedInstructionPc = _instructionPC;
      _softDeferredImpliedOpcode = _opcode;
  }

  private void ApplySoftDeferredBit()
  {
      if (!_softDeferredImpliedOp) return;

      // Apply flags immediately (matches VICE timing)
      P &= 0x3D;
      P |= (byte)(_softBitOperand & 0xC0);
      if ((A & _softBitOperand) == 0) P |= 0x02;

      // Restore PC and _pc to opcode+3
      _pc = (ushort)(_softDeferredImpliedInstructionPc + 3);
      _instructionPC = _pc;
      _opcode = _softDeferredImpliedOpcode;

      _softDeferredImpliedOp = false;
  }
```

Then in the main fetch loop, after `_softDeferredImpliedOp` handling, call `ApplySoftDeferredBit()` before the normal `_instructionPC = _pc;` line.

This should make the flags appear on cycle 521027 exactly as native does, and the V bit will be correct even if the address is off by one byte. The RTS delay 1 case will now execute BIT fully (flags on 521026) and avoid the soft path entirely.

Try this and let me know what the new P values look like at 521026/521027.

